{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Ovarian epithelial cancer, fallopian tube cancer, and primary peritoneal cancer are diseases in which malignant cells form in the tissue covering the ovary or lining the fallopian tube or peritoneum.","source_span":"diseases in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the tissue covering the ovary or lining the fallopian tube or peritoneum"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Ovarian epithelial cancer, fallopian tube cancer, and primary peritoneal cancer form in the same type of tissue and are treated the same way.","source_span":"form in the same type of tissue and are treated the same way"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Each ovary is about the size and shape of an almond.","source_span":"Each ovary is about the size and shape of an almond."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Primary peritoneal cancer is cancer that forms in the peritoneum and has not spread there from another part of the body.","source_span":"Primary peritoneal cancer is cancer that forms in the peritoneum and has not spread there from another part of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Women who have one first-degree relative (mother, daughter, or sister) with a history of ovarian cancer have an increased risk of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"Women who have one first-degree relative (mother, daughter, or sister) with a history of ovarian cancer have an increased risk of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"The risk of ovarian cancer is even higher in women who have two or more first-degree relatives with a history of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"This risk is even higher in women who have two or more first-degree relatives with a history of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Hereditary ovarian cancer makes up about 20% of all cases of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"Hereditary ovarian cancer makes up about 20% of all cases of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"There are three hereditary patterns of ovarian cancer: ovarian cancer alone, ovarian and breast cancers, and ovarian and colon cancers.","source_span":"There are three hereditary patterns: ovarian cancer alone, ovarian and breast cancers, and ovarian and colon cancers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"A risk-reducing oophorectomy is the removal of healthy ovaries so that cancer cannot grow in them, and in high-risk women this procedure has been shown to greatly decrease the risk of ovarian cancer.","source_span":"risk-reducing oophorectomy (the removal of healthy ovaries so that cancer cannot grow in them). In high-risk women, this procedure has been shown to greatly decrease the risk of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","statement":"Signs and symptoms of ovarian, fallopian tube, or peritoneal cancer may include pain, swelling, or pressure in the abdomen or pelvis, heavy or irregular vaginal bleeding especially after menopause, vaginal discharge, a lump in the pelvic area, and gastrointestinal problems such as gas, bloating, or constipation.","source_span":"Pain, swelling, or a feeling of pressure in the abdomen or pelvis. Vaginal bleeding that is heavy or irregular, especially after menopause."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"How does family history affect a woman's risk of ovarian cancer?","answer":"Having one first-degree relative (mother, daughter, or sister) with ovarian cancer increases risk; the risk is even higher with two or more first-degree relatives with a history of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"What proportion of ovarian cancer cases are hereditary, and what are the three hereditary patterns?","answer":"About 20% of ovarian cancer cases are hereditary. The three patterns are ovarian cancer alone, ovarian and breast cancers, and ovarian and colon cancers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What is a risk-reducing oophorectomy and what effect does it have in high-risk women?","answer":"It is the surgical removal of healthy ovaries so cancer cannot grow in them. In high-risk women, it has been shown to greatly decrease the risk of ovarian cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is primary peritoneal cancer?","answer":"It is cancer that forms in the peritoneum and has not spread there from another part of the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What are common signs and symptoms of ovarian, fallopian tube, or peritoneal cancer?","answer":"Pain, swelling, or pressure in the abdomen or pelvis; heavy or irregular vaginal bleeding especially after menopause; vaginal discharge; a lump in the pelvic area; and gastrointestinal problems such as gas, bloating, or constipation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Are ovarian epithelial cancer, fallopian tube cancer, and primary peritoneal cancer treated differently from each other?","answer":"No, they form in the same type of tissue and are treated the same way."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"Ovarian germ cell tumor is a disease in which malignant cells form in the germ (egg) cells of the ovary.","source_span":"malignant (cancer) cells form in the germ (egg) cells of the ovary"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"Ovarian germ cell tumors usually occur in teenage girls or young women and most often affect just one ovary.","source_span":"Ovarian germ cell tumors usually occur in teenage girls or young women and most often affect just one ovary."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"The most common ovarian germ cell tumor is called dysgerminoma.","source_span":"The most common ovarian germ cell tumor is called dysgerminoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"Signs of ovarian germ cell tumor are swelling of the abdomen or vaginal bleeding after menopause.","source_span":"Signs of ovarian germ cell tumor are swelling of the abdomen or vaginal bleeding after menopause."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"An increased level of alpha fetoprotein (AFP) or human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG) in the blood may be a sign of ovarian germ cell tumor.","source_span":"An increased level of alpha fetoprotein (AFP) or human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG) in the blood may be a sign of ovarian germ cell tumor."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for ovarian germ cell tumor depend on the type of cancer, the size of the tumor, the stage of cancer, how the cancer cells look under a microscope, and the patient's general health.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The type of cancer. - The size of the tumor. - The stage of cancer... - The way the cancer cells look under a microscope. - The patients general health."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"Ovarian germ cell tumors are usually cured if found and treated early.","source_span":"Ovarian germ cell tumors are usually cured if found and treated early."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"There are four stages used for ovarian germ cell tumors: Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV.","source_span":"Stage I     - Stage II     - Stage III     - Stage IV"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","statement":"Staging of ovarian germ cell tumor typically involves a surgical procedure called a laparotomy to see if the cancer has spread.","source_span":"an operation called a laparotomy is done to see if the cancer has spread"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Who typically develops ovarian germ cell tumors, and how many ovaries are usually affected?","answer":"They usually occur in teenage girls or young women and most often affect just one ovary."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the most common type of ovarian germ cell tumor?","answer":"Dysgerminoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which two blood tumor markers can indicate an ovarian germ cell tumor when elevated?","answer":"Alpha fetoprotein (AFP) and human chorionic gonadotropin (HCG)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What factors affect the prognosis and treatment options for ovarian germ cell tumors?","answer":"The type of cancer, the size of the tumor, the stage of cancer, how the cancer cells look under a microscope, and the patient's general health."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"How many stages are used to classify ovarian germ cell tumors?","answer":"Four stages: Stage I, Stage II, Stage III, and Stage IV."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_2","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What surgical procedure is typically used to stage an ovarian germ cell tumor?","answer":"A laparotomy, a surgical incision into the abdominal wall, is done to check whether the cancer has spread."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"Ovarian low malignant potential tumor is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissue covering the ovary.","source_span":"abnormal cells form in the tissue covering the ovary"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"Ovarian low malignant potential tumors have abnormal cells that may become cancer but usually do not, and the disease usually remains in the ovary.","source_span":"Ovarian low malignant potential tumors have abnormal cells that may become cancer, but usually do not. This disease usually remains in the ovary."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"When disease is found in one ovary, the other ovary should also be checked carefully for signs of disease.","source_span":"When disease is found in one ovary, the other ovary should also be checked carefully for signs of disease."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"The CA 125 assay measures the level of CA 125 in the blood, and an increased CA 125 level is sometimes a sign of cancer or other condition.","source_span":"CA 125 assay : A test that measures the level of CA 125 in the blood...An increased CA 125 level is sometimes a sign of cancer or other condition."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"Prognosis and treatment options for ovarian low malignant potential tumor depend on the stage of the disease, the type of cells that make up the tumor, the size of the tumor, and the patient's general health.","source_span":"The prognosis (chance of recovery) and treatment options depend on the following: - The stage of the disease...- What type of cells make up the tumor. - The size of the tumor. - The patients general health."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"Patients with ovarian low malignant potential tumors have a good prognosis, especially when the tumor is found early.","source_span":"Patients with ovarian low malignant potential tumors have a good prognosis, especially when the tumor is found early."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"Most patients with ovarian low malignant potential tumor are diagnosed with stage I disease.","source_span":"Most patients are diagnosed with stage I disease."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"Stage I of ovarian low malignant potential tumor is divided into stage IA, stage IB, and stage IC.","source_span":"Stage I is divided into stage IA, stage IB, and stage IC."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"In stage IA, the tumor is found inside a single ovary.","source_span":"Stage IA: The tumor is found inside a single ovary."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"In stage IB, the tumor is found inside both ovaries.","source_span":"Stage IB: The tumor is found inside both ovaries."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","statement":"In stage IC, the tumor is found inside one or both ovaries and either tumor cells are found on the outside surface of one or both ovaries, or the ovary's capsule has ruptured, or tumor cells are found in peritoneal cavity fluid or peritoneal washings.","source_span":"Stage IC: The tumor is found inside one or both ovaries and one of the following is true: - tumor cells are found on the outside surface of one or both ovaries; or - the capsule (outer covering) of the ovary has ruptured (broken open); or - tumor cells are found in the fluid of the peritoneal cavity...or in washings of the peritoneum"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is an ovarian low malignant potential tumor?","answer":"It is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissue covering the ovary; these cells may become cancer but usually do not, and the disease usually remains confined to the ovary."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What does the CA 125 assay measure and what can an elevated result indicate?","answer":"It measures the level of CA 125 in the blood; an increased CA 125 level is sometimes a sign of cancer or another condition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What factors affect the prognosis and treatment options for ovarian low malignant potential tumors?","answer":"The stage of the disease, the type of cells making up the tumor, the size of the tumor, and the patient's general health."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","fact_refs":[6],"question":"At what stage are most patients with ovarian low malignant potential tumor diagnosed?","answer":"Most patients are diagnosed with stage I disease."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","fact_refs":[8,9],"question":"What distinguishes stage IA from stage IB in ovarian low malignant potential tumor?","answer":"In stage IA the tumor is found inside a single ovary, while in stage IB the tumor is found inside both ovaries."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000019_3","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What conditions define stage IC of ovarian low malignant potential tumor?","answer":"The tumor is in one or both ovaries plus at least one of: tumor cells on the outer ovarian surface, a ruptured ovarian capsule, or tumor cells found in peritoneal cavity fluid or washings."}
